\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
The Internet and Product Signaling
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Date
November 7, 2013
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Experimental economists have always been fascinated with the recognized
 problems that arise due asymmetric information, most notably moral hazard.
 Within the framework of industrial organization, moral hazard occurs when
 both sellers' costs and buyers' values depend on product quality.
 The sellers know the quality of their product, while the quality cannot
 be directly observed by buyers.
 One possible solution to this problem involves signaling.
 In fact, there have been many papers analyzing the possible equilibria
 that may arise when signaling is an option.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

However, I do not believe their framwork can be readily applied to today's
 modern market institutions that have arisen due to the widespread use of
 the Internet.
 Today, individuals can leave a product review after purchasing an item,
 and this has an effect on future sales of the good.
 Then, future potential buyers of goods over the Internet are able to discern
 some of the information concerning the product's quality through these
 user reviews.
 Yet, they are limited in their ability to gather all relevant information
 since they unable to physcially examine the product or have face-to-face
 communication with the seller.
 The seller, on the other hand, can invest in the quality of the product,
 but he will be unable to relay a perfect signal to the buyer about the
 product due to the same reasons mentioned previously.
 Because of these issues, will sellers even attempt to invest in the quality
 of their products, and how will they change theire prices in the presence
 of online reviews?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In fact, Amazon has a relatively unknown program known as 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Vine
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 in which they send free items to a select group of people in exchange for
 reviews of the selected products.
 These individuals are required to submit a review online, whether it be
 positive or negative, on all the products they receive.
 Some experts claim that the reviews from Vine participants could be positively
 biased since they may be more inclined to leave encouraging feedback after
 receiving the product for free.
 Yet, Amazon claims that reviews from Vine participants are actually more
 negative, on average.
 Why does Amazon send out free products just to obtain a few extra reviews?
 Well, according to the Amazon marketing department, even negatively reviewed
 items still sell better than items with no reviews.
 In order to answer some of the previously mentioned questions, I propose
 the following experimental design that models some of the major characteristics
 associated with Internet purchases, as well as Amazon's Vine program.
 The description below is associated with the controlled portion of the
 experiment.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

I will recruit a group of 25 individuals to participate in the experiment.
 Five subjects will be randomly chosen to be the sellers of similar goods,
 while the rest of the individuals will act as buyers of these goods.
 A particular trading period will involve buyers and sellers interacting
 for five continuous minutes.
 Prior to the start of each trading period, buyers and sellers will all
 be assigned a randomy determined ID number, so they can be better identified
 during trading.
 These ID numbers will change at the start of each new trading period in
 order to maintain seller anonymity and prevent reputation building.
 Furthermore, sellers will decide how many units of quality to add to their
 products, along with the asking price, before trading begins.
 Note that these units of goods are identical in all characteristics except
 the quality and price at which they sell.
 Buyers will possess the same linear utility function, and they will receive
 increased levels of utility from goods with a higher quality level.
 Also, buyers will be given the same budget of 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

experimental tokens.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 Finally, all five sellers will face the same constant marginal cost schedule
 to add additional units of quality to their goods, yet their decision in
 product quality will not be directly observed by buyers.
 Note that the profit sellers receive for a good will be equal to the price
 of the good minus their quality expenditure.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Moving on, while trading will remain continuous, the six minute trading
 period will be divided into 6 distinct one minute sessions.
 To elaborate, sellers will only be given five units of the good to sell
 at each one minute interval.
 These units will have the same level of quality that sellers chose before
 trading began, but sellers will have the opportunity to adjust their prices
 after each one minute interval.
 Also, the budget of buyers will be replinshed at each new one minute interval.
 The buyers will not be obligated to purchase any goods because they will
 receive a very small level of utility from retaining their tokens.
 Yet, they will receive far higher levels of utility by purchasing high
 quality goods.
 The compensation buyers receive will be based on the difference in the
 value they obtain from the good minus the price they pay.
 Now, after each purchase, buyers will have the option to leave a review.
 In the review, the buyers will identify the seller using the ID number,
 and they will rank the good based on scale of 1 to 5 with 5 being the highest
 rating.
 However, there will be a fixed marginal cost that buyers must pay in order
 to leave a review.
 This information will be continuously updated, so as trading continues,
 buyers will be able to see the average rating of a seller before purchasing
 a good.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The treatment of this experiment will follow the same basic design as described
 above, with a few distinct additions to capture the effect of Amazon's
 Vine program.
 At each one minute interval, one buyer and one seller is chosen at random
 in which a seller will give one unit of his good to the buyer free of charge.
 The buyer will receive utility from this good as he normally would, but
 he will be obligated to leave a review for the seller and pay the fee to
 do so.
 Eventually, I would like sellers and buyers to elect whether or not they
 wish to participate in the experimental Vine program at all, but in order
 to simplify the experiment and associated analysis, I will begin with a
 random selection.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

With my experiment, I can analyze both buyer and seller behavior.
 On the seller side, I hope to examine the impact of peer reviews on sellers
 quality selection and pricing behavior.
 Will there be any pooling or separating equilibria that arise in terms
 of quality and price? How will the introduction of the Vine program affect
 their decisions? On the buyers side, I hope to examine the impact of peer
 reviews on their buying decisions.
 Will reviewed goods, even those with a negative review, sell better than
 goods with no reviews? Will buyers even choose to pay a fee in order to
 leave a review? 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

One of the potential setbacks arising from this experimental design is that
 the associated theory necessary to make predictions will probably be incredibly
 complicated.
 To alleviate this problem, I suggest a few changes that can be made to
 the design.
 First, I can discretize the sellers choice set by having them select a
 predetermine number of price and quality bundles to better distinguish
 equilibria, as opposed to them choosing from a continuous set.
 Second, I can limit the number of sellers to two in order to easily study
 the simple duopoly case of this experiment.
 Finally, I can have sellers only choose one determinant, either price or
 quality, and then randomize the other variable.
 Any other suggestions for simplication will be greatly appreciated.
\end_layout

